# **Part A**

### **c. Describe the concept of abstract class with an example.**  
**Abstract Class:**  
A class that **cannot be instantiated** (you cannot create objects from it). It is used to provide a **base structure** for derived classes. It contains **pure virtual/abstract methods** that must be implemented by child classes.

**C++ Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Shape {          // Abstract class
public:
    virtual void area() = 0;   // Pure virtual function
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    void area() {
        cout << "Area of circle\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Circle c;
    c.area();
}
```

